1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:11,020
All aboard the Nostalgia Express as we look at the toys of our childhood, the ones we can't forget, and those that some of us never gave up.

2
00:00:11,021 --> 00:00:12,040
Pops out and fired.

3
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:20,240
From cap guns to kung fu grip, slingshots to super soakers, it's time for the real toy story.

4
00:00:21,320 --> 00:00:25,120
Now, toys on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
To 10-year-old boys, the holiday season is the most important time of the year.

6
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:49,220
To them, it's all about toys.

7
00:00:51,240 --> 00:00:53,260
Preparation for the big day is intense.

8
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:56,320
Wish list completed? Check.

9
00:00:56,321 --> 00:01:00,320
Lists distributed to parents and grandparents.

10
00:01:00,321 --> 00:01:04,320
Check. Letter-mailed to Santa.

11
00:01:04,321 --> 00:01:08,320
Check. It's a tremendous amount of work,

12
00:01:08,321 --> 00:01:12,320
but the payoff is well worth the sacrifice.

13
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:20,320
Most boys spend an inordinate amount of time daydreaming about

14
00:01:20,321 --> 00:01:21,544
trains and action figures.

15
00:01:21,545 --> 00:01:24,320
But the toys of childhood are more important than you might

16
00:01:24,321 --> 00:01:27,920
But the toys of childhood are more important than you might think.

17
00:01:27,921 --> 00:01:32,520
Play is really the work that children do.

18
00:01:32,521 --> 00:01:38,640
Through play, they learn about cooperating, fair play, they're socialized, they try on

19
00:01:38,641 --> 00:01:41,380
different roles as through action figures.

20
00:01:41,381 --> 00:01:47,720
They really become themselves in the context of and in relationship to their world.

21
00:01:47,721 --> 00:01:53,440
If play is the work kids do, then toys are the tools of their trade.

22
00:01:53,441 --> 00:02:00,240
In the year 2000, people in America spent $23 billion on such tools.

23
00:02:02,380 --> 00:02:06,260
These tools help boys deal with what it means to be a boy.

24
00:02:07,720 --> 00:02:11,440
Stimulating, or controlling, their natural dispositions.

25
00:02:13,320 --> 00:02:16,580
Toys are a boy's way to express his creative side.

26
00:02:18,020 --> 00:02:20,180
Constructively release his pent-up aggression.

27
00:02:20,181 --> 00:02:26,860
harnesses perpetual motion and satisfy his intense curiosity.

28
00:02:29,640 --> 00:02:34,120
The idea of toys really probably goes back to caveman days when you get right down to it.

29
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:38,920
You can picture little kids in a cave playing around with a bone or two.

30
00:02:39,220 --> 00:02:43,380
Maybe they had a round rock and, you know, that became the earliest form of baseball.

31
00:02:43,381 --> 00:02:53,380
Really it's with the Egyptians that the recorded toy history kind of begins.

32
00:02:53,381 --> 00:02:58,380
Ancient Egyptian boys played with many toys still popular today.

33
00:02:58,381 --> 00:03:08,380
Leather or stone balls, tops, elaborate pool toys, even board games.

34
00:03:08,381 --> 00:03:19,760
The Greeks and Romans added to their toy boxes hobby horses, dice, wooden swords, bats,

35
00:03:20,820 --> 00:03:28,580
yo-yos made by combining two wooden or metal discs and a string, and toy soldiers formed

36
00:03:28,581 --> 00:03:29,281
of clay.

37
00:03:31,880 --> 00:03:37,320
Playtime was cut short, however, during the Middle Ages, when people became more concerned

38
00:03:37,321 --> 00:03:39,780
about surviving than playthings.

39
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:43,780
If we go back, we'd all be surprised

40
00:03:43,781 --> 00:03:46,260
at just how few toys boys had.

41
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:49,320
This is in part simply because boys

42
00:03:49,321 --> 00:03:51,980
were expected to work at a young age,

43
00:03:52,420 --> 00:03:56,500
and toys were seen simply as frivolous playthings,

44
00:03:56,800 --> 00:03:58,280
mostly for the very young.

45
00:03:59,860 --> 00:04:02,560
But toys made a comeback during the Renaissance,

46
00:04:03,180 --> 00:04:05,760
when the toy manufacturing business officially began.

47
00:04:05,761 --> 00:04:14,660
Nuremberg, Germany became one of the centers of toy production.

48
00:04:14,661 --> 00:04:18,160
German artisans made all kinds of toys.

49
00:04:18,161 --> 00:04:23,320
Wheeled horses carved out of wood and delicately detailed.

50
00:04:23,321 --> 00:04:29,340
Clay or bronze knights on horseback were particularly popular.

51
00:04:29,341 --> 00:04:34,080
These toys were sold throughout Europe, but they were handmade by craftsmen and therefore

52
00:04:34,081 --> 00:04:40,917
very expensive. Only the wealthy could afford them for their children.

53
00:04:40,918 --> 00:04:42,480
Most kids played

54
00:04:42,481 --> 00:04:49,789
with toys that they or their parents made themselves. These were far more simple.

55
00:04:49,790 --> 00:04:50,490
The

56
00:04:50,491 --> 00:04:55,200
majority of toys remained homemade until the Industrial Revolution and the introduction

57
00:04:55,201 --> 00:04:56,280
of mass production.

58
00:04:59,680 --> 00:05:02,840
During the 1800s, toys became more intricate.

59
00:05:04,880 --> 00:05:07,520
Companies introduced beautifully painted tin toys

60
00:05:07,521 --> 00:05:08,840
and complex wind-ups,

61
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:12,740
appealing as much to parents as to their kids.

62
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:16,720
And an emerging middle class

63
00:05:16,721 --> 00:05:19,160
had more opportunity to buy such toys.

64
00:05:20,780 --> 00:05:23,360
Christmas was becoming a bigger gift-giving holiday

65
00:05:23,361 --> 00:05:24,680
than it had been in the past.

66
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:33,580
As machinery replaced manpower, middle-class children also found they had more time to be children.

67
00:05:33,581 --> 00:05:39,000
But this wasn't supposed to be spent in sort of idle play.

68
00:05:39,280 --> 00:05:45,380
Children were supposed to learn how to be adults

69
00:05:45,381 --> 00:05:53,120
and learn how to enter this rapid-changing world of science and technology and business.

70
00:05:53,121 --> 00:05:59,660
And so it isn't surprising that construction toys became increasingly important.

71
00:06:01,920 --> 00:06:06,560
From a parent's standpoint, what could be more constructive than a construction toy?

72
00:06:08,220 --> 00:06:12,400
To a boy, it was simply open-ended creativity.

73
00:06:14,540 --> 00:06:20,780
Probably the most celebrated construction toy in 20th century America was the erector set.

74
00:06:20,781 --> 00:06:25,880
I got my first director set in 1955 for Christmas,

75
00:06:26,220 --> 00:06:29,280
and I couldn't wait to tear the parts out of the box

76
00:06:29,281 --> 00:06:30,720
and start building my first model.

77
00:06:32,440 --> 00:06:36,500
In 1913, A.C. Gilbert, an Olympic pole vaulter

78
00:06:36,501 --> 00:06:38,000
and Yale Medical School grad,

79
00:06:38,420 --> 00:06:40,100
was making and selling magic kits.

80
00:06:41,740 --> 00:06:43,180
He lived in New Haven,

81
00:06:43,700 --> 00:06:45,480
but took many train trips to New York,

82
00:06:45,880 --> 00:06:48,340
where he peddled magic toys to department stores.

83
00:06:48,341 --> 00:06:53,260
On one trip, presto, it came to him.

84
00:06:54,140 --> 00:06:57,980
During this process, they were building electrical towers

85
00:06:57,981 --> 00:06:59,840
to wire up the electric trains.

86
00:07:00,440 --> 00:07:03,540
And he'd watch them as he rode the train back and forth,

87
00:07:03,620 --> 00:07:06,920
and he'd see how they were using metal girders,

88
00:07:07,320 --> 00:07:08,440
building these towers.

89
00:07:08,740 --> 00:07:11,100
And he got home that night, and he started cutting out

90
00:07:11,101 --> 00:07:13,000
things out of cardboard,

91
00:07:13,380 --> 00:07:17,360
and he started building models of these pieces that he had made.

92
00:07:17,361 --> 00:07:21,240
And that's where the idea for the erector set was born.

93
00:07:22,140 --> 00:07:26,440
In 1913, Gilbert introduced his first erector set

94
00:07:26,441 --> 00:07:35,000
with model plans, girders, gears, pulleys, nuts and bolts.

95
00:07:37,060 --> 00:07:37,960
Gilbert adds red,

96
00:07:38,340 --> 00:07:40,180
Hello, boys, make lots of toys.

97
00:07:41,060 --> 00:07:42,400
And they did.

98
00:07:43,260 --> 00:07:45,340
Erector's popularity continued to grow.

99
00:07:47,360 --> 00:07:49,800
Each year, the sets seemed to get bigger and bigger.

100
00:07:51,040 --> 00:07:56,960
Well, in 1931, the Gilbert Company came out with a set.

101
00:07:57,280 --> 00:07:59,620
They called it the Climax of Erector Glory.

102
00:07:59,621 --> 00:08:05,000
It came in a big wooden chest that weighed 50 pounds without any parts in it.

103
00:08:05,680 --> 00:08:09,840
It contained every part imaginable, everything the erector ever made.

104
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:12,800
It had zeppelin parts.

105
00:08:12,980 --> 00:08:15,500
It had the locomotive.

106
00:08:15,501 --> 00:08:19,400
A boy could build almost anything with the kit.

107
00:08:24,300 --> 00:08:30,000
What distinguished Erector Sets was the electric motor, first introduced in 1913.

108
00:08:31,380 --> 00:08:35,400
With it, kids in the 50s could build a motorized amusement park,

109
00:08:36,180 --> 00:08:44,160
complete with carousel, Ferris wheel, airplane ride, and a famous parachute drop.

110
00:08:45,500 --> 00:08:52,220
An erector set is unique from all other toys. It's not just one toy. You could

111
00:08:52,221 --> 00:08:56,960
build a toy one day, take it apart the next, and build something else. It was a

112
00:08:56,961 --> 00:09:00,000
never-ending toy.

113
00:09:00,001 --> 00:09:06,220
Erector sets remained popular through the 1950s. However, some kids didn't have

114
00:09:06,221 --> 00:09:12,380
the patience for nuts and bolts. Luckily for these kids, one toy would make model

115
00:09:12,381 --> 00:09:13,280
building a snap.

116
00:09:16,980 --> 00:09:22,580
The Lego Company was established in 1932 by Ola Kirk Christiansen,

117
00:09:23,340 --> 00:09:27,200
a Danish carpenter who began producing toys when home sales slumped

118
00:09:27,201 --> 00:09:28,720
during a depression in Denmark.

119
00:09:31,480 --> 00:09:35,660
Lego is the contraction of two Danish words, leg, goat,

120
00:09:35,661 --> 00:09:37,520
which means play well.

121
00:09:37,521 --> 00:09:45,540
After World War II, Chris Chanson introduced stackable plastic building blocks, called

122
00:09:45,541 --> 00:09:50,020
automatic binding bricks.

123
00:09:50,021 --> 00:09:58,520
But it wasn't until 1955 that the Lego company introduced the Lego bricks still popular today.

124
00:09:58,521 --> 00:10:06,240
What was unique about the Lego system was the way the bricks snapped and held together.

125
00:10:06,241 --> 00:10:12,240
on top and hollow tubes underneath gave children a creative license to build anything they could

126
00:10:12,241 --> 00:10:13,023
imagine.

127
00:10:13,024 --> 00:10:21,240
Today, the Lego company, located in Billund, Denmark, produces 16 billion individual

128
00:10:21,241 --> 00:10:27,470
pieces a year, much like they've been doing for more than four decades.

129
00:10:27,471 --> 00:10:29,400
A Lego brick starts as

130
00:10:29,401 --> 00:10:36,080
plastic resin called ABS, which stands for acrylonitrile butadine styrene, and their plastic

131
00:10:36,081 --> 00:10:40,460
pellets and what we do is we heat them up till they're the consistency of hot

132
00:10:40,461 --> 00:10:46,700
toothpaste then we squeeze them together with between 10 and 25 tons of force and

133
00:10:46,701 --> 00:10:57,140
mold a Lego brick. Nowhere is the potential of the Lego brick better

134
00:10:57,141 --> 00:11:04,140
demonstrated than Legoland parks.

135
00:11:04,900 --> 00:11:09,900
At this one in San Diego, California, everything is made with Lego bricks.

136
00:11:09,901 --> 00:11:13,140
Sculptures, even city dioramas.

137
00:11:13,141 --> 00:11:20,140
Now that's a lot of Lego.

138
00:11:20,141 --> 00:11:27,000
A lot of parents still think of Lego bricks as the standard

139
00:11:27,001 --> 00:11:29,640
two by four bricks that we grew up with.

140
00:11:29,641 --> 00:11:32,320
We have so many different special unique pieces

141
00:11:32,321 --> 00:11:33,660
that the kids really love.

142
00:11:35,520 --> 00:11:38,040
Lego sets have evolved to include motors

143
00:11:38,041 --> 00:11:42,500
and computer chips, buildable action heroes,

144
00:11:42,501 --> 00:11:45,020
even a Steven Spielberg movie set,

145
00:11:45,021 --> 00:11:48,180
complete with working camera and computer editing software.

146
00:11:50,020 --> 00:11:52,900
Of course, you can still get those familiar plastic blocks

147
00:11:52,901 --> 00:11:55,420
first introduced in 1955.

148
00:11:55,421 --> 00:12:02,420
As always, what you do with them is up to your imagination.

149
00:12:02,421 --> 00:12:05,420
They don't say boys will be boys for nothing.

150
00:12:05,421 --> 00:12:12,420
Next, six shooters, slingshots, and the aggressive side of boys' toys.

151
00:12:12,421 --> 00:12:18,420
John Lloyd Wright, son of architect Frank Lloyd Wright, invented Lincoln Logs in 1916.

152
00:12:18,421 --> 00:12:25,420
His inspiration came from an earthquake-resistant construction method used by his father on a hotel in Japan.

153
00:12:25,421 --> 00:12:28,500
Toys will return on Modern Marvels.

154
00:12:31,060 --> 00:12:34,360
If construction toys help boys unlock creativity,

155
00:12:35,320 --> 00:12:37,860
other toys are meant to diffuse pent-up energy.

156
00:12:43,460 --> 00:12:45,520
Boys and girls are different.

157
00:12:45,740 --> 00:12:48,060
We are just inherently different as mammals.

158
00:12:48,380 --> 00:12:49,820
You know, we're wired differently.

159
00:12:50,980 --> 00:12:54,500
Boys tend to gravitate towards power and conflict type of play.

160
00:12:55,420 --> 00:13:03,660
Symbolizing ultimate power, toy weapons have always been popular with boys.

161
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:09,120
In the Middle Ages, toy swords and bows were used as teaching aids.

162
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:15,020
Boys mimicked what real men did, hunting and fighting.

163
00:13:16,360 --> 00:13:18,400
Soon enough, they would be using the real thing.

164
00:13:20,940 --> 00:13:24,860
Through the 1800s, however, toy weapons took on a different role.

165
00:13:25,420 --> 00:13:32,860
By the 20th century, no longer were they the tools that Dad used at work,

166
00:13:33,060 --> 00:13:35,320
unless your father happened to be Roy Rogers.

167
00:13:35,900 --> 00:13:38,220
And you'll always be ready for anything.

168
00:13:39,120 --> 00:13:41,880
Ask for Ideal's new Roy Rogers quickshooter hat.

169
00:13:43,480 --> 00:13:49,480
Instead, they became instruments by which boys diffused excessive and aggressive energy.

170
00:13:49,760 --> 00:13:52,280
Okay, Frippery, this is the end of the line.

171
00:13:52,281 --> 00:14:02,064
Many of our fondest memories are strapping on our holsters and drawing on our friends in mock shootouts at the O.K.

172
00:14:02,065 --> 00:14:02,765
Corral.

173
00:14:05,120 --> 00:14:13,220
The Cap Gun, the pride and joy of so many boys of the 1950s, was actually born toward the end of the Civil War,

174
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:17,300
when gun manufacturers switched to making toy guns.

175
00:14:17,301 --> 00:14:23,980
For realism, these companies introduced gunpowder caps that exploded when struck.

176
00:14:25,660 --> 00:14:28,640
This is the Buffalo Bill Cap Gun from the 1890s.

177
00:14:28,720 --> 00:14:33,440
His name is engraved here on the side, and it just fires a single, single shot cap.

178
00:14:33,540 --> 00:14:38,980
You just pull the hammer back, put your cap in the chamber there, and away she goes.

179
00:14:40,740 --> 00:14:45,140
Drawing on the popularity of radio, movie, and later television westerns,

180
00:14:45,141 --> 00:14:49,140
Cap gun sales shot up in the new century.

181
00:14:49,820 --> 00:14:55,040
This was my absolute favorite cap gun during my cowboy and Indian days when I was about 12 years old.

182
00:14:55,720 --> 00:15:02,620
This thing has just practically the exact weight of a real six-shooter, beautifully crafted.

183
00:15:03,280 --> 00:15:08,960
It has a pop-open cylinder here, shoots roll caps, never misfired.

184
00:15:09,060 --> 00:15:11,900
This was a trusty gun. You could count on it getting the job done.

185
00:15:11,901 --> 00:15:16,600
Cap guns are great, but they were certainly more bark than bite.

186
00:15:18,820 --> 00:15:22,280
Inevitably, some boys wanted more firepower from their toys.

187
00:15:24,740 --> 00:15:26,180
Slingshots were used for hunting.

188
00:15:26,880 --> 00:15:30,100
A kid could have a slingshot, go out and hunt squirrels or birds or whatever,

189
00:15:30,160 --> 00:15:31,180
and bring them home for dinner.

190
00:15:31,760 --> 00:15:35,440
Now that we've gotten into a world where the kid doesn't have to bring home a squirrel for dinner,

191
00:15:35,820 --> 00:15:38,220
it's become play for target practice.

192
00:15:38,560 --> 00:15:41,740
And then, of course, there's the slingshot of choice, which is the wrist rocket.

193
00:15:41,900 --> 00:15:45,900
horse chestnuts and a wrist rocket and you're good to go.

194
00:15:49,220 --> 00:15:51,280
By the early 1920s,

195
00:15:51,820 --> 00:15:54,740
boys fashioned slingshots out of rubber inner tubes

196
00:15:54,741 --> 00:15:56,200
and Y-shaped tree branches.

197
00:15:58,300 --> 00:16:01,060
But these were inaccurate and difficult to use.

198
00:16:03,380 --> 00:16:06,440
That changed with the wrist rocket slingshot

199
00:16:06,441 --> 00:16:08,120
introduced in 1953.

200
00:16:08,121 --> 00:16:14,940
looking to beef up a slingshot powerful enough to shoot snowballs at the bullies across the street

201
00:16:14,941 --> 00:16:22,300
mark and steve ellenberg and their father howard made the prototype for the first wrist rocket

202
00:16:22,301 --> 00:16:32,840
slingshot we finally came up with the first wrist brace slingshot which i'm holding in my hand now

203
00:16:32,841 --> 00:16:40,640
It had, the prongs were spaced a large distance apart, so a big snowball could go through there.

204
00:16:41,540 --> 00:16:43,780
Our mother sewed up a special pouch.

205
00:16:44,540 --> 00:16:46,700
We needed something to strap it onto the wrist.

206
00:16:46,760 --> 00:16:48,520
The first model straps onto the wrist.

207
00:16:48,940 --> 00:16:53,800
And so everybody in the family contributed something, and the dog gave his little collar.

208
00:16:54,040 --> 00:16:59,060
He was sitting on the floor looking so interested, we figured, well, he had to participate some way in this.

209
00:16:59,820 --> 00:17:02,420
The wrist brace gave the slingshot stability.

210
00:17:02,840 --> 00:17:10,520
Later, braces were made from continuous metal rods, and the dog got his collar back.

211
00:17:10,521 --> 00:17:16,540
For the firing mechanism, Howard Ellenberg chose surgical latex tubing.

212
00:17:16,541 --> 00:17:19,660
It was super stretchy and resilient.

213
00:17:19,661 --> 00:17:27,920
The slingshot proved a hit around the neighborhood, and the family decided to give sails a shot.

214
00:17:27,921 --> 00:17:32,160
The Ellenberg brothers were only 12 and 13 when they began manufacturing and demonstrating

215
00:17:32,161 --> 00:17:33,536
their invention.

216
00:17:33,537 --> 00:17:40,160
One of their first advertising campaigns included a promotional film starring

217
00:17:40,161 --> 00:17:41,555
the Ellenbergs.

218
00:17:41,556 --> 00:17:48,900
We wanted a movie to show that this was a new and exciting slingshot that could

219
00:17:48,901 --> 00:17:54,488
be like archery. We were good. We could hit almost anything.

220
00:17:54,489 --> 00:17:58,120
I shot an apple off of my father's head

221
00:17:58,121 --> 00:18:00,120
for the final shot for the movie.

222
00:18:00,620 --> 00:18:03,060
The only thing that was wrong about it

223
00:18:03,061 --> 00:18:05,420
was that the slingshot's so powerful

224
00:18:05,421 --> 00:18:08,200
that it shot, the marble went right through the apple

225
00:18:08,201 --> 00:18:09,420
and left it on his head.

226
00:18:09,560 --> 00:18:11,620
So my dad kind of jerked his head

227
00:18:11,621 --> 00:18:13,360
to have the apple fall off.

228
00:18:13,860 --> 00:18:15,160
And then, of course, the obvious happened.

229
00:18:15,320 --> 00:18:17,920
We soon realized you could not advertise

230
00:18:17,921 --> 00:18:19,380
someone shooting at someone else.

231
00:18:19,460 --> 00:18:22,300
Yeah, and we also shot a cigarette out of his mouth.

232
00:18:22,520 --> 00:18:24,600
You know, he was holding it up like that.

233
00:18:25,320 --> 00:18:27,160
Of course, that's something the brothers

234
00:18:27,161 --> 00:18:28,660
don't recommend trying at home.

235
00:18:31,860 --> 00:18:35,240
The Ellenbergs continued manufacturing and selling wrist

236
00:18:35,241 --> 00:18:37,340
rockets through high school and then college.

237
00:18:39,140 --> 00:18:42,380
Their slingshots became a worldwide phenomenon.

238
00:18:45,380 --> 00:18:49,240
Mark and Steve Ellenberg still produce TrueMark brand slingshots

239
00:18:49,241 --> 00:18:52,420
today at their manufacturing plant in Boulder, Colorado.

240
00:18:52,421 --> 00:18:57,620
At the factory, aluminum rods are cut and bent into shape.

241
00:19:01,220 --> 00:19:05,320
Plastic injection molding machine spits out thousands of handles a day.

242
00:19:06,820 --> 00:19:12,160
The rubber, plastic, leather, and aluminum pieces come together and eventually are packaged.

243
00:19:12,161 --> 00:19:25,680
The latest true mark slingshot has a 225 yard range, complete with fiber optic sights and

244
00:19:25,681 --> 00:19:30,520
recoil stabilizer bar.

245
00:19:30,521 --> 00:19:39,160
With this stabilizer and these sights, I've hit 57 hits in a row.

246
00:19:39,161 --> 00:19:41,960
I think Mark was trailing by about 42 years.

247
00:19:41,961 --> 00:19:43,180
No, no, no, no, 47.

248
00:19:43,480 --> 00:19:45,780
Okay, all right, well, I'm still up on him.

249
00:19:46,600 --> 00:19:49,760
Either way, with thousands of slingshots sold each year,

250
00:19:50,080 --> 00:19:52,720
it seems that both brothers are right on target.

251
00:19:54,140 --> 00:19:54,840
Aim.

252
00:19:55,620 --> 00:19:56,320
Fight.

253
00:19:58,700 --> 00:20:01,660
Although True Mark only advocates target practice,

254
00:20:02,540 --> 00:20:05,700
used incorrectly, a slingshot can do some damage.

255
00:20:05,701 --> 00:20:09,760
But some toys offer the best of both worlds

256
00:20:09,761 --> 00:20:13,200
Aggressive outlet and good clean fun

257
00:20:13,201 --> 00:20:17,180
Moms have sort of made a deal with the devil

258
00:20:17,181 --> 00:20:20,080
That says you can't have a BB gun and you can't have a cap gun

259
00:20:20,081 --> 00:20:21,640
But okay, a water gun

260
00:20:21,641 --> 00:20:24,040
Because I know you're not really going to get hurt with it

261
00:20:24,041 --> 00:20:28,180
Squirt guns date at least as far back as the 1920s

262
00:20:28,181 --> 00:20:32,040
This is one of the most graphic water guns ever made

263
00:20:32,041 --> 00:20:35,040
Really spacey, bright lightning bolts, red and yellow

264
00:20:35,041 --> 00:20:38,620
again made by the Daisy Company, famous for their BB guns.

265
00:20:39,180 --> 00:20:43,460
This one had a little internal bladder to hold the water,

266
00:20:43,580 --> 00:20:45,040
and that almost always dries out,

267
00:20:45,120 --> 00:20:46,920
so it's near impossible to find these working.

268
00:20:47,100 --> 00:20:49,320
But the main idea is the looks,

269
00:20:49,500 --> 00:20:52,300
so a nice scarce gun in great shape.

270
00:20:53,980 --> 00:20:55,280
After World War II,

271
00:20:55,480 --> 00:20:57,840
companies began making squirt guns out of plastic.

272
00:20:59,220 --> 00:21:00,660
But the early ones leaked,

273
00:21:01,220 --> 00:21:02,560
didn't hold enough aqua ammo,

274
00:21:02,561 --> 00:21:04,340
and certainly didn't squirt very far.

275
00:21:05,040 --> 00:21:11,040
That all changed with the introduction of the super soaker in 1990.

276
00:21:11,041 --> 00:21:17,040
With a few pumps of the pressurizing mechanism, super soakers can shoot a torrential stream of water,

277
00:21:17,041 --> 00:21:20,040
halfway around the world, it seems.

278
00:21:20,041 --> 00:21:24,040
And did it take a rocket scientist to come up with this radically improved water weapon?

279
00:21:24,041 --> 00:21:28,040
Well, yes it did.

280
00:21:28,041 --> 00:21:34,540
In 1982, Lonnie Johnson was working on the Galileo spacecraft at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory.

281
00:21:37,320 --> 00:21:42,480
One of his side projects was finding a more environmentally friendly method of refrigeration,

282
00:21:42,940 --> 00:21:47,180
using small water jets instead of ozone-damaging Freon.

283
00:21:48,120 --> 00:21:53,100
And I connected these nozzles to my bathroom sink, and I was doing some tests.

284
00:21:53,980 --> 00:21:57,720
And as I had the high-pressure water coming through the nozzle,

285
00:21:57,860 --> 00:22:01,160
I shot it across the bathroom, and I looked at the stream of water,

286
00:22:01,240 --> 00:22:03,960
and I thought that, geez, this would make a neat water gun.

287
00:22:04,060 --> 00:22:07,280
It was such a powerful stream that I thought it would really be fun to play with.

288
00:22:08,380 --> 00:22:12,240
After tinkering in his basement, Johnson came up with a prototype.

289
00:22:13,560 --> 00:22:18,360
What I did was to use a pressure bottle as a pressure vessel

290
00:22:18,361 --> 00:22:20,120
so that all the pressure would be here,

291
00:22:20,121 --> 00:22:23,660
and the water would float through conduits to a valve

292
00:22:23,661 --> 00:22:26,080
and then from the valve to the nozzle of the gun.

293
00:22:26,720 --> 00:22:29,020
And the pump is here underneath the barrel.

294
00:22:29,820 --> 00:22:32,760
The pump pressurized air inside the water reservoir.

295
00:22:33,280 --> 00:22:35,660
With a pull of the trigger valve, water rushed out.

296
00:22:37,520 --> 00:22:40,400
Most toy manufacturers thought Johnson was all wet.

297
00:22:41,480 --> 00:22:44,100
It took eight years before the Laramie Toy Company

298
00:22:44,101 --> 00:22:45,740
started selling super soakers.

299
00:22:45,741 --> 00:22:50,920
This is the gun that we ended up carrying into mass production.

300
00:22:51,340 --> 00:22:55,820
You can see the basic configuration of the gun was maintained, even the angle of the handle here.

301
00:22:57,380 --> 00:23:02,080
In only two years, the super soaker became the most popular summer toy in America.

302
00:23:04,740 --> 00:23:10,380
Some of today's super soakers hold one and a half gallons of water and have a range of 50 feet.

303
00:23:10,381 --> 00:23:17,380
Lonnie Johnson made millions on his invention, but he didn't retire.

304
00:23:17,381 --> 00:23:23,380
At his company in Georgia, he's working on everything from hair care products to advanced energy systems.

305
00:23:23,381 --> 00:23:28,380
He's still working to make the world a better place.

306
00:23:28,381 --> 00:23:34,380
But to millions of kids, he's already made the world better and wetter.

307
00:23:34,381 --> 00:23:41,380
Next, fasten your seatbelts as we look at the wheeled wonders of toys.

308
00:23:41,381 --> 00:23:47,380
In 1951, Mr. Potato Head became the first toy to be advertised on television.

309
00:23:47,381 --> 00:23:52,380
Toys will return on Modern Marvels.

310
00:23:52,381 --> 00:23:58,380
Boys are naturally full of energy, bundles of perpetual motion,

311
00:23:58,381 --> 00:24:04,380
which might explain their fascination with anything that rolls, flies, floats, scoots, or slides.

312
00:24:04,381 --> 00:24:12,200
probably since the introduction of the wheel kids have wanted to know how do we get around

313
00:24:12,201 --> 00:24:17,320
how do we move faster it's just a natural it seems to be a very natural way of playing

314
00:24:17,321 --> 00:24:21,540
it's something that men don't seem to outgrow

315
00:24:21,541 --> 00:24:30,280
here at the atlantic city antique show some of the most popular booths sell classic transportation

316
00:24:30,281 --> 00:24:30,981
toys.

317
00:24:30,982 --> 00:24:40,100
Toys that would have been popular for boys of the 1870s, 80s, that period, would have been

318
00:24:40,101 --> 00:24:41,311
like wheeled vehicles.

319
00:24:41,312 --> 00:24:45,220
This one is articulated, so you're going to get the simulation that the

320
00:24:45,221 --> 00:24:47,584
horse is galloping.

321
00:24:47,585 --> 00:24:57,040
But nothing on wheels has captured the imagination of boys more than trains.

322
00:24:57,041 --> 00:25:00,800
symbols of speed and power

323
00:25:00,801 --> 00:25:05,900
real steam toys appeared as early as 1870

324
00:25:05,901 --> 00:25:15,760
really great ones had a little boiler here in which you would put water underneath there was

325
00:25:15,761 --> 00:25:22,860
a reservoir in which to put alcohol and you unscrew this and put the alcohol in and it tells

326
00:25:22,861 --> 00:25:30,860
the young fellow just light each of these wicks and when he had kind of a little raging inferno

327
00:25:30,861 --> 00:25:36,540
going there then put the train on the track you'd give it a little bump with your finger and the

328
00:25:36,541 --> 00:25:42,540
locomotive and the cars would start to chug along very much like a live steam train on the railroad

329
00:25:43,500 --> 00:25:51,580
only they forgot to put a throttle on these all this little guy knew was the hotter the water got

330
00:25:51,581 --> 00:25:57,320
the more steam that was generated, the faster he went round and round and round the tree

331
00:25:57,321 --> 00:26:00,160
until, whoops, it flipped off the track.

332
00:26:00,840 --> 00:26:03,020
The alcohol spilled out of the tank.

333
00:26:03,240 --> 00:26:05,620
The little flames in there lit the alcohol.

334
00:26:06,540 --> 00:26:09,360
Up went the carpet, in turn the Christmas tree,

335
00:26:09,600 --> 00:26:13,420
and I guess the whole family went out together.

336
00:26:13,540 --> 00:26:14,660
It must have been really colorful.

337
00:26:17,540 --> 00:26:20,480
Fortunately for carpets and Christmas trees everywhere,

338
00:26:21,380 --> 00:26:25,300
Joshua Cowan introduced an electric train that proved less flammable.

339
00:26:27,220 --> 00:26:30,480
In 1900, Cowan sold electric fans.

340
00:26:31,200 --> 00:26:33,680
But when the weather cooled off, so did business.

341
00:26:35,180 --> 00:26:40,400
He thought he might incorporate the fan motor into a kind of moving window display.

342
00:26:41,980 --> 00:26:45,720
He basically made these, they looked like wooden cigar boxes.

343
00:26:45,721 --> 00:26:48,260
and he stuck his fan motor underneath

344
00:26:48,261 --> 00:26:51,800
and he took some strips of brass and he made a track

345
00:26:51,801 --> 00:26:54,240
and these things would spin around the window,

346
00:26:54,740 --> 00:26:58,220
attracting all kinds of attention and sell goods.

347
00:26:59,220 --> 00:27:01,820
When the electric train proved more popular

348
00:27:01,821 --> 00:27:03,460
than the products it was demonstrating,

349
00:27:04,220 --> 00:27:07,480
Cowan incorporated under his middle name, Lionel,

350
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:10,480
and the famous Lionel Train Company was born.

351
00:27:12,580 --> 00:27:14,940
More realistic trains soon followed.

352
00:27:15,720 --> 00:27:23,160
Over the next 50 years, Lionel introduced authentic steamers, streamliners, and diesels.

353
00:27:25,160 --> 00:27:28,340
Giant holiday displays drew kids like flies.

354
00:27:32,620 --> 00:27:34,880
And trains were only half the fun.

355
00:27:37,060 --> 00:27:42,960
Lionel offered animated accessories from water tanks to bascule bridges.

356
00:27:42,961 --> 00:27:46,260
log and coal loaders

357
00:27:46,261 --> 00:27:48,940
to cranes.

358
00:27:53,520 --> 00:27:56,560
Trains gave boys a world of their own to control.

359
00:27:57,740 --> 00:28:00,480
What kid didn't want to be a railroad engineer?

360
00:28:00,660 --> 00:28:01,360
I couldn't wait.

361
00:28:03,100 --> 00:28:04,960
Over the last hundred years,

362
00:28:04,961 --> 00:28:08,440
the Lionel Company has sold over 50 million trains.

363
00:28:08,441 --> 00:28:14,040
trains were popular in the 20th century

364
00:28:14,041 --> 00:28:17,120
but really it was the century of the automobile

365
00:28:17,121 --> 00:28:19,760
and kids toys reflected that

366
00:28:19,761 --> 00:28:23,640
when you get into sort of modern times

367
00:28:23,641 --> 00:28:25,480
you see the fascination with cars

368
00:28:25,481 --> 00:28:26,720
especially in America

369
00:28:26,721 --> 00:28:28,680
and kids always want to drive

370
00:28:28,681 --> 00:28:31,220
I mean really that's one of the first things you see

371
00:28:31,221 --> 00:28:34,240
for millions of males

372
00:28:34,241 --> 00:28:35,900
the first car they ever owned

373
00:28:35,901 --> 00:28:37,140
was a matchbox

374
00:28:37,141 --> 00:28:50,000
In the early 1950s, Lesney Products, an electrical parts manufacturer, started making miniature royal coaches as souvenirs for the crowning of Queen Elizabeth II.

375
00:28:52,980 --> 00:28:59,500
When the London-based company sold over one million coaches, they decided to concentrate on toys.

376
00:29:00,380 --> 00:29:02,880
But what really put Lesney in the driver's seat

377
00:29:02,881 --> 00:29:05,740
was a simple brass road roller prototype

378
00:29:05,741 --> 00:29:07,740
made by partner Jack O'Dell.

379
00:29:08,680 --> 00:29:11,340
Matchbox started as a school project.

380
00:29:11,940 --> 00:29:14,500
Jack O'Dell made the first Matchbox

381
00:29:14,501 --> 00:29:15,840
for his daughter to bring to school.

382
00:29:16,300 --> 00:29:18,660
And the school said that you had to be able to put

383
00:29:18,661 --> 00:29:20,020
whatever you brought in for show and tell

384
00:29:20,021 --> 00:29:21,320
inside of a Matchbox.

385
00:29:21,440 --> 00:29:24,280
So he created a small car that she brought to school

386
00:29:24,281 --> 00:29:25,200
and shared with the class.

387
00:29:25,201 --> 00:29:28,080
So, interestingly, Matchbox started as a girl's toy.

388
00:29:29,500 --> 00:29:39,760
In 1952, Lesney started producing small, very detailed vehicles and packaging them in boxes about the size of Matchboxes.

389
00:29:42,660 --> 00:29:46,560
Priced under a buck, they were cool and affordable.

390
00:29:47,840 --> 00:29:50,940
It didn't take long for Matchbox to become king of the road.

391
00:29:52,660 --> 00:29:55,980
Matchboxes made over 12,000 different kinds of cars.

392
00:29:56,980 --> 00:30:00,120
Each year, the company unveils 75 new cars.

393
00:30:01,400 --> 00:30:03,160
Collectors love these numbered vehicles.

394
00:30:04,200 --> 00:30:06,640
Although they may have been purchased for a few cents,

395
00:30:07,480 --> 00:30:09,920
rare cars now go for thousands of dollars.

396
00:30:12,420 --> 00:30:14,600
Die-hard collectors are typically male,

397
00:30:15,300 --> 00:30:17,180
but there are exceptions to the rule.

398
00:30:18,380 --> 00:30:22,460
My son is 50 this year, and when he was about 13,

399
00:30:22,461 --> 00:30:27,260
his room was cluttered with cars, to me was cluttered.

400
00:30:27,261 --> 00:30:31,260
So I got together all the cars, all the matchbox cars that were in his room,

401
00:30:31,660 --> 00:30:34,820
and I sold them to somebody who wanted them,

402
00:30:34,940 --> 00:30:37,060
not knowing that they were of any value.

403
00:30:37,940 --> 00:30:43,320
And every year at Christmas, I have to listen to how I gave away his matchbox cars.

404
00:30:43,660 --> 00:30:45,340
So I've been still looking for them.

405
00:30:46,620 --> 00:30:48,460
I always thought he kind of got over it,

406
00:30:48,480 --> 00:30:50,380
but I have found out through the years that he didn't.

407
00:30:50,381 --> 00:30:54,540
And I feel badly about it, but that's what I do.

408
00:30:55,600 --> 00:30:58,460
Although not all collectors have such thoughtful mothers,

409
00:30:59,180 --> 00:31:01,360
they seem to share other characteristics.

410
00:31:02,940 --> 00:31:06,580
Men who grew up with Matchbox cars are now buying them for their kids.

411
00:31:08,220 --> 00:31:11,180
Dad doesn't seem to mind handing over the keys to this car.

412
00:31:13,380 --> 00:31:17,940
Next, we'll go through basic training with a world's first action figure.

413
00:31:17,941 --> 00:31:22,940
Matchbox has sold over 3 billion cars since 1952.

414
00:31:22,941 --> 00:31:26,940
The top-selling vehicle is the Model A Ford.

415
00:31:26,941 --> 00:31:30,940
Toys will return on Modern Marvels.

416
00:31:30,941 --> 00:31:36,940
I am ready to attack with Johnny Seven OMA, the one-man army.

417
00:31:36,941 --> 00:31:41,940
Boys have always been captivated by the dangerous exploits of the fighting man.

418
00:31:41,941 --> 00:31:46,940
The Greeks had toy soldiers carved from wood or fired ceramic.

419
00:31:46,941 --> 00:31:53,900
ceramic. By the late 1500s, wealthy European children played with miniature

420
00:31:53,901 --> 00:32:01,880
soldiers cast in lead. Toymakers made molds into which they poured molten lead.

421
00:32:01,881 --> 00:32:09,380
After cooling, the toys were often brilliantly painted. In 1760, Johann

422
00:32:09,381 --> 00:32:16,560
Hilpert, a German tinsmith, started producing less expensive tin soldiers.

423
00:32:16,940 --> 00:32:26,580
Although cast much the same way as lead soldiers, these were flat, easier to paint, faster to manufacture, and therefore far more affordable.

424
00:32:28,200 --> 00:32:31,920
Tin soldiers invaded Europe, then captured the United States.

425
00:32:33,800 --> 00:32:42,900
In the 20th century, the Markstoy Company, among others, brought us plastic platoons of green army men manufactured by the bucket load.

426
00:32:46,940 --> 00:32:54,204
But no toy soldier caught the attention of kids like G.I.

427
00:32:54,205 --> 00:32:57,900
Joe, the first action figure.

428
00:32:59,280 --> 00:33:01,140
Just don't call him a doll.

429
00:33:02,020 --> 00:33:05,900
I think our culture never wanted boys to play with dolls.

430
00:33:06,100 --> 00:33:09,580
So you don't say, hi, son, here's a doll.

431
00:33:09,700 --> 00:33:11,480
You say, you know, here's G.I. Joe.

432
00:33:12,060 --> 00:33:13,240
And it's all marketing.

433
00:33:13,360 --> 00:33:15,820
It really is marketing, and it's how we perceive it.

434
00:33:16,940 --> 00:33:19,300
But action figures aren't dolls,

435
00:33:20,200 --> 00:33:23,520
and certainly not G.I. Joe, the manliest of all action figures.

436
00:33:26,320 --> 00:33:29,020
G.I. Joe was recruited in 1964

437
00:33:29,021 --> 00:33:32,700
when Hasbro introduced a series of 12-inch characters

438
00:33:32,701 --> 00:33:36,540
that embodied the heroic spirit of the American soldier.

439
00:33:38,220 --> 00:33:39,960
Originally, when the line came out,

440
00:33:40,060 --> 00:33:42,840
there were the four basic services represented.

441
00:33:43,000 --> 00:33:46,700
You had your action soldier, action sailor, action pilot,

442
00:33:46,701 --> 00:33:49,940
and Action Marine, and then a whole bunch of accessories

443
00:33:49,941 --> 00:33:51,820
specific for each service.

444
00:33:53,480 --> 00:33:56,540
Yes, Joe had as many accessories as Barbie,

445
00:33:57,080 --> 00:33:59,020
but these weren't sweater sets.

446
00:33:59,280 --> 00:34:01,740
These were weapons, scuba suits, and vehicles.

447
00:34:04,980 --> 00:34:08,680
By 1965, Joe became the most popular toy

448
00:34:08,681 --> 00:34:10,620
of kids 5 to 12 in America.

449
00:34:12,600 --> 00:34:14,440
But Hasbro knew that they had to innovate

450
00:34:14,441 --> 00:34:16,980
to basically keep the line fresh continuously.

451
00:34:18,060 --> 00:34:22,360
In 1965, Hasbro introduced the first black action soldier.

452
00:34:23,480 --> 00:34:27,300
In 1967, talking figures were introduced,

453
00:34:27,520 --> 00:34:30,480
which you pulled a string on the G.I. Joe's chest.

454
00:34:30,481 --> 00:34:32,700
I have got a proper present for you.

455
00:34:34,580 --> 00:34:37,480
But with the anti-war movement of the late 1960s,

456
00:34:38,620 --> 00:34:40,480
Joe became more of an adventurer.

457
00:34:41,120 --> 00:34:42,480
The adventure team featured characters

458
00:34:42,481 --> 00:34:47,935
characters with lifelike hair and beards. A lot of people call these fuzzy head G.I.

459
00:34:47,936 --> 00:34:48,636
Joes.

460
00:34:49,260 --> 00:34:56,160
In the 70s, Hasbro also introduced Kung Fu grip, and the idea of a grip that you could lock

461
00:34:56,161 --> 00:35:01,260
together like that, first of all, it played great, but then it was a catchphrase, I think,

462
00:35:01,261 --> 00:35:04,940
for a whole generation of boys that lives on to this day in pop culture.

463
00:35:05,940 --> 00:35:10,940
G.I. Joe became a casualty in the late 1970s when interest waned,

464
00:35:11,380 --> 00:35:14,020
and Hasbro stopped producing 12-inch figures.

465
00:35:14,980 --> 00:35:21,960
But in 1982, Joe re-emerged, a real American hero, this time a bit smaller.

466
00:35:24,100 --> 00:35:26,360
But you can't keep a good man down.

467
00:35:28,740 --> 00:35:32,360
In 1996, Joe stood tall once more.

468
00:35:32,361 --> 00:35:35,740
when Hasbro decided to release 12-inch figures again.

469
00:35:38,220 --> 00:35:40,080
Although intended for collectors,

470
00:35:40,940 --> 00:35:43,220
Joe proved even more popular among kids.

471
00:35:44,780 --> 00:35:47,420
Since September 11, 2001,

472
00:35:47,920 --> 00:35:51,140
the iconic action figure has enjoyed renewed popularity.

473
00:35:52,520 --> 00:35:55,820
I think those events reinforced

474
00:35:55,821 --> 00:36:00,000
what the core brand values of G.I. Joe

475
00:36:00,001 --> 00:36:04,240
are intended to do, and that's celebrate American heroism.

476
00:36:05,040 --> 00:36:06,200
Mission accomplished.

477
00:36:06,700 --> 00:36:07,420
What part, man?

478
00:36:11,840 --> 00:36:14,260
Next, out-of-this-world toys

479
00:36:14,261 --> 00:36:16,800
and the inquisitive side of human nature.

480
00:36:19,040 --> 00:36:22,840
In 1993, the Barbie Liberation Organization

481
00:36:22,841 --> 00:36:26,920
swapped the voice chips of 300 G.I. Joe figures and Barbie dolls,

482
00:36:27,720 --> 00:36:29,180
then returned them to be sold.

483
00:36:30,000 --> 00:36:31,420
The rewired Barbies yelled,

484
00:36:31,640 --> 00:36:32,680
Eat lead, Cobra,

485
00:36:33,340 --> 00:36:34,660
while G.I. Joe said,

486
00:36:35,200 --> 00:36:36,120
Let's go shopping.

487
00:36:37,680 --> 00:36:40,260
Toys will return on Modern Marvels.

488
00:36:41,860 --> 00:36:44,660
Few things capture the imagination of children

489
00:36:44,661 --> 00:36:49,140
more than the cold, dark, and unknown void of outer space.

490
00:36:50,900 --> 00:36:52,820
Long before the space race,

491
00:36:53,700 --> 00:36:56,220
flying saucers, aliens, and robots

492
00:36:56,221 --> 00:36:57,740
were the rage of the universe.

493
00:36:57,741 --> 00:37:06,820
space toys are attractive to children because they're about taking the child out of the present

494
00:37:06,821 --> 00:37:14,280
and giving the child not only a sense of freedom from the oppressions of mom and dad and that

495
00:37:14,281 --> 00:37:20,480
obnoxious older sister of his but they're also about giving the child a kind of a sense of his

496
00:37:20,481 --> 00:37:26,940
own personal future a world that will be different perhaps better than that of his parents

497
00:37:26,941 --> 00:37:33,600
Many a well-meaning mother more concerned about lack of space than outer space

498
00:37:33,601 --> 00:37:37,420
discarded cherished toys as kids grew up.

499
00:37:39,180 --> 00:37:43,800
Fortunately, people like Ted Haake sell the toys and collectibles of our past.

500
00:37:45,240 --> 00:37:48,240
Here, memories fill shelf after shelf.

501
00:37:48,880 --> 00:37:54,220
The concept of space toys began with the comic strip Buck Rogers in the 25th century.

502
00:37:54,221 --> 00:38:01,220
In a capsule, the story is that Buck goes to sleep for 500 years,

503
00:38:01,221 --> 00:38:04,340
and when he wakes up, the world has changed, needless to say.

504
00:38:06,140 --> 00:38:10,320
This is a Buck Rogers wind-up toy by the Lewis Marks Company.

505
00:38:10,800 --> 00:38:12,680
It's titled Rocket Police Patrol.

506
00:38:13,100 --> 00:38:15,880
It's from towards the late 1930s.

507
00:38:16,540 --> 00:38:17,780
It's a wind-up toy.

508
00:38:18,420 --> 00:38:23,140
It travels along, and actually back here there's a little flint wheel

509
00:38:23,141 --> 00:38:25,560
and a piece of flint, and when the toy goes,

510
00:38:25,660 --> 00:38:29,160
it shoots a blast of sparks out the rocket exhaust back here.

511
00:38:32,120 --> 00:38:36,540
During the 1950s, UFO sightings and popular TV shows

512
00:38:36,541 --> 00:38:40,080
such as Tom Corbett's Space Cadet and Space Patrol

513
00:38:40,081 --> 00:38:42,680
helped launch a slew of space toys.

514
00:38:45,280 --> 00:38:49,760
The main export from Japan after World War II was tin toys.

515
00:38:49,761 --> 00:38:52,320
They made airplanes and cars and boats,

516
00:38:52,321 --> 00:38:58,080
but by far the most popular with collectors today that command the most money are the Japanese space toys.

517
00:38:58,081 --> 00:39:05,920
The workmanship's quite good, but what you're really paying for with the space toys is the imagination and the beautiful lithography.

518
00:39:05,921 --> 00:39:14,320
These toys were made by hand, assembled by hand, sometimes out of whatever scrap they had around.

519
00:39:14,321 --> 00:39:20,320
Sometimes if you open these toys up, you look inside and you might find a beer can label or something to that effect inside.

520
00:39:20,321 --> 00:39:22,320
They used whatever they had available.

521
00:39:26,560 --> 00:39:31,820
This is a robot from the mid-1950s, mid-late 50s, called the Radar Robot.

522
00:39:32,080 --> 00:39:33,960
And it is battery-powered.

523
00:39:34,280 --> 00:39:36,420
It has a small robot head remote control.

524
00:39:36,640 --> 00:39:38,500
You put the batteries in, which to power the toy.

525
00:39:39,140 --> 00:39:41,420
And it has a little light that lights up, and he walks.

526
00:39:42,080 --> 00:39:46,020
And he's a little mechanical man, made in Japan.

527
00:39:47,020 --> 00:39:48,180
This toy is 1200.

528
00:39:50,320 --> 00:39:52,620
A collector might need this one for his collection,

529
00:39:52,621 --> 00:39:54,860
or he might like the lithography of the toy.

530
00:39:54,861 --> 00:39:56,200
If I had the original box, the price

531
00:39:56,201 --> 00:39:58,000
could be two to three times more than this.

532
00:40:06,640 --> 00:40:08,540
I've been looking for that robot for 10 years.

533
00:40:08,541 --> 00:40:10,880
It's the famous radar robot.

534
00:40:10,881 --> 00:40:12,300
You found it here in Atlantic City.

535
00:40:12,301 --> 00:40:13,580
I found it in Atlantic City.

536
00:40:13,581 --> 00:40:15,640
My good friend.

537
00:40:15,641 --> 00:40:17,940
We choose to go to the moon in this...

538
00:40:17,941 --> 00:40:23,300
In 1961, John F. Kennedy announced our intention to send an American to the moon.

539
00:40:24,900 --> 00:40:29,500
For kids across the country, fantasy would soon be reality.

540
00:40:31,100 --> 00:40:38,820
In the 1960s, kids became more interested in real spacemen, such as John Glenn and Flash Gordon.

541
00:40:38,821 --> 00:40:45,920
And when we finally made it to the moon in 1969,

542
00:40:46,640 --> 00:40:50,160
that inspired, again, a whole bunch of space capsules

543
00:40:50,161 --> 00:40:53,120
as well as an exact replica of the lunar lander.

544
00:40:55,020 --> 00:40:58,780
TV shows including Lost in Space, My Favorite Martian,

545
00:40:58,781 --> 00:41:02,020
and Star Trek continued to inspire toys.

546
00:41:03,740 --> 00:41:08,020
But one movie would forever alter the galaxy of space toys.

547
00:41:08,820 --> 00:41:15,880
We had an opportunity in 77 to view a trailer from a new movie coming out called Star Wars.

548
00:41:16,140 --> 00:41:21,100
It was something that we had never seen, and it was something that, wow, we really want to be a part of this universe.

549
00:41:22,520 --> 00:41:26,700
The movie was filled with rich characters and imaginative space vehicles.

550
00:41:27,240 --> 00:41:34,780
To make sure that we could come up with manageable vehicles, we had to try to come up with a new type of figure

551
00:41:34,781 --> 00:41:41,120
and determined that 3 3⁄4-inch scale seemed to be just the perfect size.

552
00:41:41,300 --> 00:41:43,540
It allowed us to get the realism that we needed,

553
00:41:43,740 --> 00:41:47,700
and it also allowed us to develop vehicles that were a perfect fit.

554
00:41:49,660 --> 00:41:54,360
In 1978, Kenner released the original Star Wars toys,

555
00:41:54,780 --> 00:41:56,800
which sold faster than Lightspeed.

556
00:41:57,560 --> 00:42:02,040
The product line started with only 12 figures, 3 vehicles, and a play set,

557
00:42:02,041 --> 00:42:07,100
and evolved into over 100 figures

558
00:42:07,101 --> 00:42:09,740
by the time we did the first trilogy.

559
00:42:11,640 --> 00:42:14,340
Today, there are action figures of all kinds.

560
00:42:15,000 --> 00:42:16,260
They're more detailed than ever,

561
00:42:16,760 --> 00:42:18,700
some humorous, some grotesque.

562
00:42:20,460 --> 00:42:23,620
Collectors account for 40% of action figure sales.

563
00:42:24,700 --> 00:42:26,680
The classic figures, such as Star Wars,

564
00:42:26,681 --> 00:42:29,380
are more popular among toy collectors than ever.

565
00:42:30,100 --> 00:42:31,860
This figure can basically, in this package,

566
00:42:32,040 --> 00:42:36,640
sell anywhere from about $750 up to about $1,500,

567
00:42:36,641 --> 00:42:38,680
depending on the condition.

568
00:42:38,681 --> 00:42:40,780
Figures can get as expensive as $5,000.

569
00:42:45,280 --> 00:42:47,180
But who are collectors?

570
00:42:47,181 --> 00:42:49,660
And why do they spend thousands of dollars on toys

571
00:42:49,661 --> 00:42:51,720
from their childhood?

572
00:42:51,721 --> 00:42:54,420
It's often said that people are trying to recapture

573
00:42:54,421 --> 00:42:58,000
their youth with toy collecting.

574
00:42:58,001 --> 00:42:59,540
Actually, I don't think that's quite true.

575
00:42:59,541 --> 00:43:01,500
I think the people that collect the toys

576
00:43:01,501 --> 00:43:03,400
are people that are still in touch with their youth,

577
00:43:03,460 --> 00:43:05,820
and there's a very big difference in those two perspectives.

578
00:43:06,680 --> 00:43:09,820
I think probably the one thing we all have in common

579
00:43:09,821 --> 00:43:13,140
is we're kids that just never quite got around to growing up,

580
00:43:13,560 --> 00:43:16,520
and at least for me, I hope that I never do.

581
00:43:20,040 --> 00:43:23,360
Whether proudly displayed, collecting dust in the attic,

582
00:43:23,760 --> 00:43:26,300
or casualties of a garage sale years ago,

583
00:43:27,100 --> 00:43:30,800
the toys of our childhood often represent a less complicated time.

584
00:43:31,500 --> 00:43:38,500
But toys ultimately are really not so much about the fun they bring or the memories they evoke.

585
00:43:38,501 --> 00:43:45,500
At the end of the day, really, the piece of plastic or the piece of metal or the piece of wood is completely irrelevant.

586
00:43:45,501 --> 00:43:49,500
It's what the child's mind brings to that object.

587
00:43:49,501 --> 00:43:54,500
It's really what the child creates in his or her imagination.

